></button></div></div></div></a></figure></div><p>Ok, this one is about AI =
too, but you probably should read it. </p><p>As you know, I spend essential=
ly all of my time these days thinking about this, and working with it. And =
I talk to a lot of people about it, too. I see the usual bifurcation - some=
 folks are happy and embracing it, and some hate it and want nothing to do =
with it. We=E2=80=99d expect that - that=E2=80=99s the usual =E2=80=9Cwhy n=
ot/what if=E2=80=9D dynamic from anything disruptive, and, since this is VE=
RY disruptive, that effect is stronger. </p><p>What=E2=80=99s really intere=
sting though, is that there are folks who are absolutely =E2=80=9Cred pille=
d=E2=80=9D: getting a ton done, super engaged, often super tired, but overa=
ll very effective. I had a meeting today with someone I turned onto agentic=
 coding a few weeks ago - he excitedly told me about turning Claude code lo=
se on his marketing and sales, rebuilt a much better site, kicked off a bun=
ch of sales processes he wouldn=E2=80=99t have been able to start, and more=
.</p><p><span>So, some people are getting it, and some aren=E2=80=99t. Why?=
 Some of it is attitude, but not all, or even most. It=E2=80=99s something =
about the </span><em>shape</em><span> of their work. I suspect this is actu=
ally the key thing, and I want to try to explain what I mean.</span></p><p>=
The people with the tailwind have found surfaces that agents can grab onto.=
 They often have things they can iterate and experiment on. They have data =
that the model can get to easily. Their workflows decompose into discrete o=
bservable steps. Their documentation is structured well enough that a machi=
ne can navigate it. Their interfaces are clean enough that something other =
than a human can drive them. </p><p>And there=E2=80=99s an aspect of =E2=80=
=9Crecursion=E2=80=9D to what successful folks are doing. They often stop a=
nd look at their own process and ask what is or isn=E2=80=99t working, and =
feed that back into the model. The folks getting traction are finding ways =
to shape the work so agents can help and are looking to increase leverage (=
as we discussed last week). The folks running into headwinds are doing thei=
r jobs the same way they always did, maybe with a chatbot open in another t=
ab. Incremental, not compounding.</p><p><span>One good analogy to this is s=
hipping containers, believe it or not. When Malcom McLean standardized the =
metal box in the 1950s, it looked like a logistics story. A box is a box. B=
ut what the container actually did was standardize the </span><em>interface=
</em><span> between production and transport so completely that it restruct=
ured global trade. The revolution wasn=E2=80=99t the ship =E2=80=94 it was =
what the ship could now </span><em>assume</em><span> about the cargo. That =
assumption, that everything was in standard boxes, meant that the work coul=
d be automated - instead of humans manually carrying cargo on and off ships=
, we could build automated shipyards, and cranes, and trucks and train cars=
 that all handled that standard container. </span></p><p>Once the assumptio=
n was baked in everywhere, everything compounded. The leverage came from th=
e assumption, not the container. </p><p>The interesting thing here is that,=
 to some degree, we=E2=80=99ve tried to do that with software, but it=E2=80=
=99s much harder. Containers are easy - literally a physical shape. Softwar=
e, intention, cognition - comes in many shapes. It=E2=80=99s been much hard=
er to find standards - we do things like build database schemas or standard=
s like HTML so that, in some domain, computers have a chance of doing stand=
ard, scaled work. </p><p>That=E2=80=99s starting to change with agents. Any=
 time content is =E2=80=9Cplumbed in=E2=80=9D so that the agent can see it,=
 they can be effective. Having a system =E2=80=9Cshaped=E2=80=9D for agents=
 the way containers are =E2=80=9Cshaped=E2=80=9D for shipping makes all of =
it more effective - models do well with small, composable, well-defined pie=
ces, and they don=E2=80=99t mind doing =E2=80=9Ctoil=E2=80=9D to have them =
work. A model is perfectly happy with html and ftp to do a presentation, bu=
t a human wouldn=E2=80=99t be. </p><p>Once the idea of containers was in th=
e world, there was a powerful motivation for everyone to adapt to that syst=
em - it was much more efficient, and it took over pretty quickly. The same =
is going to happen with software and all =E2=80=9Cintellectual worker=E2=80=
=9D tasks. Having a program, data source, or process that models can=E2=80=
=99t access is going to be more or less like shipping something outside of =
a container - maybe possible, far more expensive, and hard to convince ever=
yone to do. </p><p>So the folks that are =E2=80=9Cgetting it=E2=80=9D see t=
his new world and are able to shift their work to it. They=E2=80=99re happy=
 - the new tools make them more effective, and often help them do something=
 that before wasn=E2=80=99t possible at all. </p><p>Here=E2=80=99s the part=
 that makes me genuinely uneasy about this moment though: the people who do=
n=E2=80=99t see this, don=E2=80=99t know they=E2=80=99re being left behind.=
 That=E2=80=99s the thing that doesn=E2=80=99t show up anywhere. Their metr=
ics look fine. They=E2=80=99re shipping their projects, hitting their numbe=
rs, doing their jobs. The gap is invisible because the scoreboard hasn=E2=
=80=99t changed yet.</p><p>We=E2=80=99ve seen this movie before, though. Bo=
rders was succeeding by bookstore metrics right until it wasn=E2=80=99t. Ne=
wspapers were profitable deep into the era when they were structurally fini=
shed. The signal shows up not as failure, but as a quiet ceiling that keeps=
 the good times from getting better. And the people who noticed the interne=
t early didn=E2=80=99t look dramatically different from the ones who didn=
=E2=80=99t =E2=80=94 until suddenly they did. Organizations today are going=
 on as though nothing has changed - but trust me, once you=E2=80=99ve been =
able to work at higher speed with agentic tools, that form of work will see=
m as archaic as newspapers suddenly did when the internet arrived. </p><p>I=
 don=E2=80=99t think this is about hustle or keeping up or being an early a=
dopter. I think it=E2=80=99s a structural question about interfaces: what d=
oes it take so that your work is =E2=80=9Con agent=E2=80=9D the way we used=
 to say =E2=80=9Conline=E2=80=9D? That answer is different for every person=
 and role. But it=E2=80=99s the urgent question now, and most of us are not=
 yet asking it.</p><p>The race is on. It just doesn=E2=80=99t look like a r=
ace yet from where most people are standing. But it=E2=80=99s a race nevert=
heless. </p><p data-attrs=3D"{&quot;url&quot;:&quot;https://sundaylettersfr=
