iv></div><div class=3D"comment-body expanded"><p><span>From my observations=
, there is a number of factors (simplification, evidently), with one being =
the most prominent.</span></p><p><span>Reasons for not trying new stuff:</s=
pan></p><p><span>* High comfort threshold</span></p><p><span>* Ignorance (w=
ilful or forced)</span></p><p><span>* Barred access (lack of time, lack of =
resources)</span></p><p><span>* Strategic wait (let the stuff stabilise)</s=
pan></p><p><span>* Strategic spite (let their bubble pop)</span></p><p><spa=
n>Reasons to try new stuff:</span></p><p><span>* Curiosity</span></p><p><sp=
an>* Real needs or current incomfort</span></p><p><span>Reasons for trying =
and succeeding</span></p><p><span>* Overlap between tech and real needs</sp=
an></p><p><span>* High grit</span></p><p><span>* High intelligence and/or e=
xperience</span></p><p><span>* Ability to circumvent existing limitations</=
span></p><p><span>* Ability to detect and exploit value</span></p><p><span>=
I'd say most software engineers went through an active vibecoding phase wit=
h subpar results, myself included. That laid the basis for a strategic wait=
 in optimistic personalities, or strategic spite in pessimistic ones. At so=
me point when things aren't going well or when curiosity wins these enginee=
rs try again, with new knowledge and better toolset.</span></p><p><span>The=
 ones that try again and are either sufficiently stubborn, sufficiently int=
elligent or sufficiently experienced strike the goldmine where most others =
fail. Handholding by someone with demonstratable results also works.</span>=
</p><p><span>And there are others that either can't simply put understand /=
 reeducate themselves to fit in the new world or really cling to the strate=
gic spite despite all evidence. In some cases it's so blunt that it's almos=
t like a self-preservation instinct that is failing to fire.</span></p></di=
